## Movebank client (`mb-client`)

The client uses Movebank's direct-read service:
`https://www.movebank.org/movebank/service/direct-read`. It supports anonymous
requests for public metadata and authenticated requests for account-specific or
restricted data.

```rust
pub struct Client { /* reqwest::Client, limiter, cache, auth */ }

impl Client {
    pub fn new(auth: Auth) -> Result<Self, MovebankError>;
    pub fn from_env() -> Result<Self, MovebankError>;

    pub async fn list_studies(&self) -> Result<Vec<Study>, MovebankError>;
    pub async fn get_study(&self, study_id: i64) -> Result<Study, MovebankError>;
    pub async fn get_individuals(&self, study_id: i64) -> Result<Vec<Individual>, MovebankError>;
    pub async fn get_deployments(&self, study_id: i64) -> Result<Vec<Deployment>, MovebankError>;
    pub async fn get_events(&self, q: &EventQuery) -> Result<Vec<Fix>, MovebankError>;

    pub async fn get_csv_after_accepting(
        &self,
        params: &[(&str, &str)],
        terms: &[u8],
    ) -> Result<String, MovebankError>;
    pub fn has_accepted_license(
        &self,
        study_id: i64,
        terms: &[u8],
    ) -> Result<bool, MovebankError>;
}

pub struct EventQuery {
    pub study_id: i64,
    pub individuals: Option<Vec<String>>,
    pub sensor_type_id: Option<i64>, // e.g. GPS is 653 in the documented example
    pub start: Option<NaiveDateTime>,
    pub end: Option<NaiveDateTime>, // formatted for Movebank as yyyyMMddHHmmssSSS
}
```

Movebank's CSV timestamps don't include a timezone. Since some fields can be
UTC, GPS time, or local time, the client keeps them as `NaiveDateTime` rather
than guessing an offset. Event-query bounds use Movebank's
`yyyyMMddHHmmssSSS` format. A fix is marked as an outlier when `visible` is
`false`.

License acceptance is stored per study, user scope, and terms hash. The caller
should use `get_csv_after_accepting` only after the user has read and agreed to
the terms.

## Taxonomy and study browser

```rust
// mb-taxonomy
pub enum TaxonClass { Aves, Mammalia, Reptilia, Amphibia, Fish, Invertebrate, Other, Unknown }

pub struct TaxonResolution { pub raw: String, pub canonical: Option<String>,
                             pub class: TaxonClass, pub confidence: f32, pub source: TaxonSource }

pub fn split_taxon_field(raw: &str) -> Vec<&str>;            // comma/semicolon separated
pub fn normalize_name(name: &str) -> String;                // spacing, case, and authorship
pub async fn resolve_taxon(name: &str, overrides: &Overrides, gbif: Option<&GbifClient>)
    -> Result<TaxonResolution, GbifError>;                   // manual override first, then GBIF
pub fn primary_class(rs: &[TaxonResolution]) -> TaxonClass; // majority, or Other if there is no majority
```

Manual corrections live in a CSV with `normalized_name`, `canonical`, and
`class` columns. Load it with `Overrides::from_path`; class values use the
snake-case names from `TaxonClass`, such as `mammalia`. `GbifClient` caches
matches for 24 hours. Pass `None` when you only want to use manual overrides.

```rust
// mb-neglect
pub struct StudyRow { pub id: i64, pub name: String, pub taxon_raw: String,
    pub class: TaxonClass, pub n_individuals: Option<u32>, pub n_locations: Option<u64>,
    pub duration_days: Option<f64>, pub downloadable: bool, pub neglect_score: f64 }

pub struct Filter { pub class: Option<TaxonClass>, pub min_individuals: Option<u32>,
    pub min_days: Option<f64>, pub downloadable: bool }

impl StudyRow {
    pub fn duration_days(first: Option<NaiveDateTime>, last: Option<NaiveDateTime>) -> Option<f64>;
    pub async fn build_study_table(client: &Client, overrides: &Overrides,
        gbif: Option<&GbifClient>) -> Result<Vec<StudyRow>, NeglectError>;
    pub fn class_share(rows: &[StudyRow]) -> HashMap<TaxonClass, f64>;
    pub fn neglect_score(rows: &mut [StudyRow], weights: Option<&HashMap<TaxonClass, f64>>)
        -> Result<(), NeglectError>;
    pub fn filter_studies(rows: &[StudyRow], filter: &Filter) -> Vec<StudyRow>;
}
```

`build_study_table` gets taxon names from each study's individual metadata; it
doesn't treat `taxon_ids` as names. It makes one individual-metadata request per
study, so the first run may take a while. The Movebank client caches responses
for later runs. The command prints unresolved taxon values so they can be
reviewed or added to the override file.

Class shares are calculated from classified studies only. A score uses the
within-class percentiles for individual count and duration, averages whichever
of those values are available, and applies either a supplied class weight or
the inverse class share. Scores are then scaled so the highest score is 100.
Rows with no usable metrics or an `Unknown` class receive zero.

### Binary: `mbbrowse`

Run the browser from the workspace:

```powershell
cargo run -p mbbrowse -- --class reptilia --min-individuals 5 --min-days 90 --downloadable --out studies.csv
```

You can also pass `--overrides data/taxon_overrides.csv` to use manual
corrections, or `--no-gbif` to resolve names from overrides only. The CSV is
sorted by neglect score, highest first. The optional web view is not part of
this command yet.

Before calling the browser ready, check it against public Movebank data. It
should load and cache the full study list, classify at least 90% of studies,
and give an otherwise comparable study in an underrepresented class a higher
score.

---

## Planned: tag-effect checker (`mb-tageffect`)

The tag-effect checker will compare movement in the first few days after
deployment with a later baseline. It will describe any change as a
post-deployment signal, not as proof that the tag caused it.

```rust
// loader.rs
pub async fn load_study_tracks(client: &MovebankClient, study_id: i64,
    individuals: Option<&[String]>, sensor: &str) -> Result<Vec<Track>>;
pub struct Track { pub individual_id: String, pub deploy_start: NaiveDateTime, pub fixes: Vec<Fix> }

// cleaning.rs
pub fn drop_invalid_fixes(t: &mut Track);                    // null coords, outlier flag, dupes, pre-deployment
pub fn flag_speed_outliers(t: &Track, max_speed_ms: f64) -> Vec<bool>;
pub fn detect_schedule_changes(t: &Track) -> Vec<ScheduleChange>;   // median fix interval shifts
pub fn resample(t: &Track, interval: Duration) -> Track;     // common interval so periods compare

// geo.rs
pub fn haversine_m(a: (f64, f64), b: (f64, f64)) -> f64;
pub fn bearing_rad(a: (f64, f64), b: (f64, f64)) -> f64;

// metrics.rs
pub struct DailyMetrics { pub individual_id: String, pub day_since_deploy: i32,
    pub daily_displacement_m: f64, pub mean_speed_ms: f64, pub mean_step_m: f64,
    pub mean_abs_turn_rad: f64, pub nsd_m2: f64, pub fix_success: f64 }
pub fn step_lengths(t: &Track) -> Vec<f64>;
pub fn turning_angles(t: &Track) -> Vec<f64>;
pub fn daily_metrics(t: &Track, expected_fixes_per_day: Option<f64>) -> Vec<DailyMetrics>;

// model.rs
pub enum Metric { Speed, DailyDisplacement, MeanStep, TurnAngle, FixSuccess }
pub struct IndividualEffect { pub individual_id: String, pub metric: Metric,
    pub effect: f64, pub ci_low: f64, pub ci_high: f64, pub n_early: usize, pub n_base: usize }

/// Early = days 0..n; baseline = days >= baseline_start. Effect = log ratio of means.
/// Bootstrap by resampling days (block bootstrap to respect autocorrelation).
pub fn first_n_vs_rest(d: &[DailyMetrics], metric: Metric, n_days: i32,
                       baseline_start: i32, n_boot: usize, seed: u64) -> Vec<IndividualEffect>;

/// Per-day-since-deployment profile with bootstrap band across individuals. 
/// Optional penalized-spline version via nalgebra.
pub fn recovery_profile(d: &[DailyMetrics], metric: Metric, n_boot: usize, seed: u64) -> Profile;
pub fn estimate_recovery_day(p: &Profile) -> Option<i32>;

// meta.rs
/// DerSimonian-Laird random-effects pooling across individuals.
pub fn pool_effects(e: &[IndividualEffect]) -> PooledEffect;   // estimate, CI, tau^2, I^2

// report.rs
pub enum Flag { StrongEarlySignal, SeasonConfounded, ScheduleChanged, JuvenileDispersalPossible, LowN }
pub fn study_summary(effects: &[IndividualEffect], pooled: &PooledEffect, ctx: &StudyContext) -> Summary;
pub fn render_report(s: &Summary, out_dir: &Path) -> Result<PathBuf>;   // markdown/HTML + plotters PNGs
```

### Binary: `mbtagcheck`

```
mbtagcheck --study 123456 --accept-license --sensor gps --n-days 7 \
           --baseline-start 14 --metrics speed daily-displacement fix-success --out report/
```

### Acceptance

- Run the tool end to end on a real study after the user accepts its license.
- Test the model with simulated data: recover a known three-day reduction within
  the confidence interval, and keep the false-positive rate near its expected
  level when there is no effect. Use a fixed random seed.
- Detect changes in fix schedules and compute metrics from resampled tracks.
- Include the confounder caveat in every report.

## What remains

The client, taxonomy resolver, study-scoring library, and `mbbrowse` command are
in place. The next steps are to validate the browser against live public study
metadata, then build the tag-effect checker:

1. Add geometry and cleaning helpers, with tests using hand-calculated tracks.
2. Calculate per-day movement metrics and handle changes in fix schedules.
3. Implement individual-level comparisons, bootstrap intervals, and the
   random-effects meta-analysis; validate them with simulated data.
4. Generate the report and add the `mbtagcheck` command with the explicit
   license-acceptance flow.
5. Consider the optional R/Python model only after the pure-Rust version works.

## Limitations to keep in mind

- Taxon names in Movebank metadata can be incomplete or inconsistent. Keep the
  raw values and maintain the manual override file.
- Movebank's license flow or API attributes may change; verify them against the
  current documentation.
- Deployment timestamps can be missing or approximate. Any fallback to the
  first recorded fix should be visible in the report.
- A post-deployment change can't be separated from natural behavior without a
  suitable control group. Reports must not present the association as causation.